(ARG-11 — the Accessing User Arguments example (LANGUAGE.md:4420-4426),
 verbatim: a count guard, `arguments's first` declared inside the
 comma-continued branch, and an Otherwise arm. Run WITH an argument, so
 the greeting arm is the one taken.
 Print writes a line each, so "Hello, " and the name land on separate
 lines — that is the I/O section's rule, not this section's, and the
 example makes no claim about it either way.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox ARG-11.vox -o p && ./p Ada
 Args: Ada
 expected output:
   Hello, 
   Ada
)
(Get the first argument passed by the user)
If arguments's count is greater than 1 then,
    a text called username is arguments's first,
    Print "Hello, ",
    Print the username.
Otherwise,
    Print "Hello, World!".
